    <rdfs:label>P001: AHoJ_DB - was_built_from - Protein_ligand_interaction</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/was_built_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_ligand_interaction"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB was assembled by processing 468,293 biologically relevant protein-ligand interactions drawn from BioLiP2.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We use a previously developed application (AHoJ) to perform a comprehensive search for apo-holo pairs for 468,293 biologically relevant protein-ligand interactions across 27,983 proteins.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P002">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P002: AHoJ_DB - uses_input_interactions_from - BioLiP2</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/uses_input_interactions_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/BioLiP2"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The biologically relevant protein-ligand interactions used as query input for building AHoJ-DB are taken from the redundant set of BioLiP2, excluding peptide and nucleic acid ligands.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The biologically relevant interactions used as search input are taken from the redundant set of the BioLiP2 database, excluding interactions between proteins and peptides or nucleic acids, and also interactions where the protein structure is not assigned a UniProt AC.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P003">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P003: AHoJ_DB - uses_residue_mappings_from - SIFTS</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/uses_residue_mappings_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/SIFTS"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB maps binding residues across candidate structures using SIFTS residue-level mappings.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We then conducted separate searches for each of these interactions, by specifying the ligand in the query structure, detecting its binding residues (i.e., pocket) based on a cut-off distance threshold (4.5 Å), and then mapping these residues — through SIFTS — across other structures (candidates) that are assigned the same UniProt AC in the PDB.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P004">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P004: AHoJ_DB - searches_within - PDB</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/searches_within"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/PDB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB searches for apo and holo candidate structures within the PDB, restricted to structures sharing the same UniProt accession as the query.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The mapped residues are grouped into a mapped candidate pocket and used to detect ligands within the same cut-off distance threshold used to determine binding residues for the ligand of the query structure, using an updated version of AHoJ.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P005">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P005: AHoJ_DB - groups_candidates_by - UniProt_accession</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/groups_candidates_by"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB groups all candidate structures within the same UniProt accession, enabling comparison of apo and holo states for the same protein sequence.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The database captures the variability of proteins with identical sequences, thereby exposing the agents responsible for the observed differences in geometry.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P006">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P006: AHoJ_DB - reports - Pocket_RMSD</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/reports"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_RMSD"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB reports pocket RMSD as one of the geometric metrics computed for each mapped candidate pocket.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>AHoJ-DB reports general structure metrics, relevant-chain metrics such as TM-score, RMSD and allosteric factors, and pocket metrics such as mapped and observed residues, SASA, molecular surface, volume, b-factor, radius of gyration and pocket RMSD.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P007">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P007: AHoJ_DB - reports - B_factor</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/reports"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/B_factor"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB reports per-atom b-factor values for pocket atoms, which reflect local atomic disorder and differ systematically between apo and holo pockets.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Per-atom average b-factor values (b-factor per-atom) are also in support of the claim that apo pockets are less ordered overall (26% difference with holo pockets while resolution has an 8.6% difference).</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P008">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P008: AHoJ_DB - can_support - Machine_learning</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Machine_learning"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB provides a large annotated dataset suitable for training and evaluating machine-learning models for binding site prediction and structural biology.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The authors conclude that AHoJ-DB is, to their knowledge, the most comprehensive database for apo and holo structures to date. They state that it can support protein structure analysis, machine-learning tool development, drug design and docking, LBS predictor training and testing, validation of cryptic binding sites, detection of allosteric effects, order-to-disorder studies and folding dynamics.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P009">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P009: AHoJ_DB - can_support - Drug_design</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Drug_design"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB can support drug design and docking studies by providing paired apo and holo structures with annotated pocket geometry.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The authors conclude that AHoJ-DB can support protein structure analysis, machine-learning tool development, drug design and docking, LBS predictor training and testing, validation of cryptic binding sites, detection of allosteric effects, order-to-disorder studies and folding dynamics.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P010">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P010: AHoJ_DB - can_be_used_to_detect - Cryptic_binding_site</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_be_used_to_detect"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Cryptic_binding_site"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB can be used to validate and detect cryptic binding sites that are normally obscured by conformational changes between apo and holo states.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>There are ongoing efforts to expand the druggable proteome by revealing binding sites that are normally obscured by conformational changes that occur between the apo and holo states. AHoJ-DB can support validation of cryptic binding sites.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P011">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P011: AHoJ_DB - can_support - LBS_predictor</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_support"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/LBS_predictor"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB was partly motivated by the need for large annotated benchmarks on which LBS predictors can be trained and tested.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Our initial motivation to assemble a dataset of apo-holo structures was indeed driven by the lack of sizable benchmarks on which we could test and improve our machine learning LBS predictor.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P012">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P012: AHoJ_DB - enables_comparison_of - Conformational_variability</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/enables_comparison_of"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>AHoJ-DB captures conformational variability across structures with identical sequences, exposing geometric differences not attributable to sequence change.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The database captures the variability of proteins with identical sequences, thereby exposing the agents responsible for the observed differences in geometry. We report several metrics for each annotated pocket.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Apo_form as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P013">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P013: Apo_form - is_larger_in - Pocket_RMSD</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_larger_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_RMSD"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Apo pockets show a 40% higher pocket RMSD than holo pockets (1.47 Å vs 0.96 Å in Subset 1), indicating greater geometric deviation when aligned onto the query pocket.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>A difference of 40% is observed in pocket RMSD, showing that apo pockets have consistently higher RMSD compared to holo when aligned onto the query pocket, hinting towards the existence of cryptic sites.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P014">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P014: Apo_form - is_higher_in - B_factor</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_higher_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/B_factor"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Apo pockets have 26% higher per-atom b-factor than holo pockets, indicating that ligand-free binding sites are less ordered at the atomic level.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Per-atom average b-factor values (b-factor per-atom) are also in support of the claim that apo pockets are less ordered overall (26% difference with holo pockets while resolution has an 8.6% difference).</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P015">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P015: Apo_form - is_smaller_in - Pocket_volume</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_smaller_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_volume"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Apo pockets appear 24% smaller in volume than holo pockets on average, consistent with ligand-induced pocket expansion.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>There is also a noticeable difference in measured pocket volume with apo pockets appearing smaller overall (24% difference), which is less pronounced in total SASA values.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P016">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P016: Apo_form - indicates_disorder_in - Unobserved_residues</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/indicates_disorder_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Unobserved_residues"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Apo structures show 138% more unobserved binding residues than holo structures on average, indicating that apo pockets are substantially less ordered and harder to resolve crystallographically.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Indeed, in Table 1, we see a 138% difference in the average number of unobserved residues (Unobserved MBR %) between apo and holo sites. This difference is further pronounced when considering the least disordered apo and holo pockets among an entry (see unobserved_rsd_min in Table S1), where the difference rises to 182%.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P017">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P017: Apo_form - is_smaller_in - Subset1</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_smaller_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Subset1"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Only 41% of entries in Subset 1 have at least one apo result, reflecting an inherent PDB bias toward holo structures.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Subset 1 yielded 451,408 entries across 27,162 UniProt ACs, out of which, 41% had at least one apo state as a result (185,193 paired entries).</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P018">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P018: Apo_form - may_expose - Cryptic_binding_site</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/may_expose"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Cryptic_binding_site"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The higher pocket RMSD in apo structures hints at the existence of cryptic binding sites that are occluded in the holo state.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>A difference of 40% is observed in pocket RMSD, showing that apo pockets have consistently higher RMSD compared to holo when aligned onto the query pocket, hinting towards the existence of cryptic sites.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Binding_pocket as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P019">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P019: Binding_pocket - can_occur_at - Interface_pocket</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_occur_at"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Interface_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>About 24% of binding sites in AHoJ-DB occur at the interface of two or more chains, forming multi-chain pockets. These interface pockets are explicitly included and annotated.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Analysis of the database shows that about 24% of the binding sites occur at the interface of two or more chains and that less than 50% of the total binding sites processed have an apo form in the PDB.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P020">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P020: Binding_pocket - is_assigned_per - Pocket_centric_classification</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_assigned_per"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_centric_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The apo or holo classification is assigned per pocket rather than per chain or whole structure, because a single chain can contain multiple binding sites in different states.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>It became clear that assigning apo or holo status to an entire chain, let alone a structure, can be vague and potentially misleading, in the presence of multiple binding sites on the same chain. In this sense, an apo or holo classification should be assigned based on a reference point that must be established first: a pocket or group of residues in a protein structure. A pocket-centric view is therefore practical for clarifying the apo-holo status.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P021">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P021: Binding_pocket - enables_comparison_of - Conformational_variability</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Binding_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/enables_comparison_of"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Having access to mapped candidate pockets that correspond to the same sequence region as the query enables geometric comparisons beyond the binary apo/holo classification, revealing conformational variability.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Having access to the mapped candidate pockets which correspond to the same sequence part as the query pocket, enables comparisons beyond the apo/holo classification. For this reason, we collect an extensive number of metrics that can help discern observed differences in geometry between two states.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Pocket_RMSD as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P022">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P022: Pocket_RMSD - varies_between - Apo_holo_pair</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_RMSD"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/varies_between"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Pocket RMSD systematically differs between apo and holo entries: apo pockets are 40% higher in RMSD than holo pockets when aligned onto the query, providing a quantitative measure of structural divergence.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>A difference of 40% is observed in pocket RMSD, showing that apo pockets have consistently higher RMSD compared to holo when aligned onto the query pocket, hinting towards the existence of cryptic sites.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P023">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P023: Pocket_RMSD - provides_benchmark_for - LBS_predictor</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_RMSD"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/provides_benchmark_for"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/LBS_predictor"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Pocket RMSD enables evaluation of LBS predictors beyond pass/fail classification by quantifying the geometric closeness between predicted and true pockets in paired apo-holo structures.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The idea behind this is that testing and training on holo targets, while straightforward, is not a realistic scenario, as the holo pocket is an easy target for prediction – or docking – but often differs from the apo form of the same protein sequence.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Protein_ligand_interaction as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P024">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P024: Protein_ligand_interaction - requires - UniProt_accession</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_ligand_interaction"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Only protein-ligand interactions where the protein structure has an assigned UniProt accession are used as input, because the methodology searches for candidates within the same UniProt AC.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We also discarded interactions where the protein structure is not assigned a UniProt AC, since our methodology searches for candidates within a UniProt AC.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P025">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P025: Protein_ligand_interaction - excludes - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_ligand_interaction"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/excludes"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Each protein-ligand interaction is itself a holo state by definition, and is not considered in the holo results of its own entry. It serves only as the reference query pocket.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>It is worth clarifying that while the entry itself is a holo interaction, it is not considered in the holo results of the given entry. The entries can be retrieved by searching through AHoJ-DB for a PDB code, ligand CCD name, UniProt AC, or a combination of these identifiers.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P026">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P026: Protein_ligand_interaction - is_most_abundant_in - Zinc_ligand</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Protein_ligand_interaction"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_most_abundant_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Zinc_ligand"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Zinc (ZN) is the most represented ligand in Subset 2 by total number of interaction sites, with 42,137 sites across 4,237 UniProt ACs.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The top 15 ligands are displayed in Table 2. ZN leads with 4,237 UniProt ACs, 15,715 structures, 32,014 chains and 42,137 sites in total entries.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — BioLiP2 as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P027">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P027: BioLiP2 - excludes - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/BioLiP2"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/excludes"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>BioLiP2 interactions involving peptides or nucleic acids as ligands are excluded from the AHoJ-DB input, retaining only small-molecule protein-ligand interactions.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We collected the biologically relevant protein-ligand interactions from BioLiP2 (redundant set, retrieved on 23.12.2023) and we discarded interactions between proteins and peptides or nucleic acids, and also interactions where the protein structure is not assigned a UniProt AC.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P028">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P028: BioLiP2 - provides_benchmark_for - AHoJ_DB</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/BioLiP2"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/provides_benchmark_for"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AHoJ_DB"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>BioLiP2 serves as the authoritative source of biologically relevant ligand interactions that define what counts as a valid holo query state in AHoJ-DB.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We used biologically relevant interactions as starting points; we consider any detected ligand to be valid. The idea behind this is that even if these ligands are part of crystal packing contacts or crystallographic agents, they will affect the local geometry despite their questionable biological relevance.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Residue_mapping as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P029">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P029: Residue_mapping - uses_residue_mappings_from - SIFTS</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Residue_mapping"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/uses_residue_mappings_from"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/SIFTS"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Binding residues identified in the query structure are transferred to candidate structures via SIFTS residue-level mappings, enabling pocket-level comparison across structures.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We then conducted separate searches for each of these interactions, by specifying the ligand in the query structure, detecting its binding residues (i.e., pocket) based on a cut-off distance threshold (4.5 Å), and then mapping these residues — through SIFTS — across other structures (candidates) that are assigned the same UniProt AC in the PDB.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P030">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P030: Residue_mapping - requires - UniProt_accession</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Residue_mapping"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The residue mapping step requires that both query and candidate structures share the same UniProt accession, ensuring that mapped residues correspond to the same protein sequence region.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The search logic groups candidates by UniProt AC. Only structures assigned the same UniProt AC as the query are considered as candidates for residue mapping and apo/holo classification.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Pocket_centric_classification as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P031">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P031: Pocket_centric_classification - resolves_ambiguity_in - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_centric_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/resolves_ambiguity_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>A pocket-centric view resolves the ambiguity in apo/holo assignment that arises when a single chain has multiple binding sites, some bound and some unbound.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>It became clear that assigning apo or holo status to an entire chain, let alone a structure, can be vague and potentially misleading, in the presence of multiple binding sites on the same chain. A pocket-centric view is therefore practical for clarifying the apo-holo status.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P032">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P032: Pocket_centric_classification - classifies - Apo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_centric_classification"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/classifies"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>A mapped candidate pocket is classified as apo if no ligands are detected in its vicinity, regardless of what other pockets on the same chain contain.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The mapped candidate pockets are classified as apo or holo, depending on whether any ligands were detected in their vicinity, excluding solvent.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Glucose_ligand as subject (high apo coverage example)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P033">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P033: Glucose_ligand - has_higher_apo_coverage_than - Zinc_ligand</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glucose_ligand"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_higher_apo_coverage_than"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Zinc_ligand"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Glucose (GLC) has 62-75% apo coverage across UniProt, structures and chains in Subset 2, far exceeding zinc (ZN) at 22-28%, illustrating strong ligand-specific variation in apo availability.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The top 15 ligands are displayed in Table 2. Apo-paired coverage varies strongly between ligands. Glucose shows 62% UniProt coverage, while ZN shows only 22%, demonstrating that the likelihood of finding an apo form differs markedly by ligand type.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P034">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P034: Glucose_ligand - is_most_abundant_in - Apo_holo_pair</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Glucose_ligand"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_most_abundant_in"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Among the top 15 ligands, glucose has the highest apo-coverage percentage (73% of chains have at least one apo result), making it the best-represented ligand for apo-holo paired studies.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The paper reports high coverage for glucose with 62% UniProt, 75% structures, 71% chains and 73% sites coverage in Subset 2.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — LBS_predictor as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P035">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P035: LBS_predictor - often_relies_on - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/LBS_predictor"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/often_relies_on"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>LBS prediction software is often trained and tested on single holo structures as representatives, not accounting for conformational diversity of the binding site or the rest of the protein.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The testing and training of LBS prediction software is often performed on a single holo structure as a representative, not taking into account the conformational diversity of the binding site or the rest of the protein.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P036">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P036: LBS_predictor - requires - Apo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/LBS_predictor"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Realistic LBS prediction should target apo forms, since holo pockets are easy targets and often differ geometrically from their apo counterparts, making holo-only training potentially misleading.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The idea behind this is that testing and training on holo targets, while straightforward, is not a realistic scenario, as the holo pocket is an easy target for prediction – or docking – but often differs from the apo form of the same protein sequence.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Conformational_variability as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P037">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P037: Conformational_variability - is_biased_toward - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_biased_toward"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>The average number of pockets, structures, and chains per entry is consistently 95-100% higher for holo states than apo, indicating an inherent PDB bias toward holo structures.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>The average number of pockets, structures, and chains per entry is consistently higher for holo states – 95% to 100% difference – throughout all subsets compared to apo, indicating an inherent bias within the PDB towards holo structures.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P038">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P038: Conformational_variability - varies_between - Apo_holo_pair</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Conformational_variability"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/varies_between"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Conformational variability between apo and holo structures is not captured by a single protein structure, making paired datasets essential for understanding geometry differences.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>A single protein structure is rarely sufficient to capture the conformational variability of a protein. Both bound and unbound (holo and apo) forms of a protein are essential for understanding its geometry and making meaningful comparisons.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — AlphaFold as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P039">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P039: AlphaFold - is_biased_toward - Holo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AlphaFold"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/is_biased_toward"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Holo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Proteins with larger documented conformational variability in the PDB produce lower quality AlphaFold models, suggesting AlphaFold captures holo-like states more faithfully than apo states.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Saldaño et al. compared AlphaFold models of 91 proteins with a curated set of apo-holo structure pairs and concluded that the models resemble the holo form 70% of the time, while proteins with larger documented conformational variability in the PDB produced lower quality AlphaFold models.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P040">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P040: AlphaFold - requires - Apo_holo_pair</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/AlphaFold"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_holo_pair"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Evaluating AlphaFold model quality relative to experimental structures requires curated apo-holo pairs as the reference benchmark, since single-state comparisons are insufficient.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Saldaño et al. compared AlphaFold models of 91 proteins with a curated set of apo-holo structure pairs and concluded that the models resemble the holo form 70% of the time.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Subset1 / Subset2 as subjects
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P041">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P041: Subset2 - requires - Unobserved_residues</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Subset2"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Unobserved_residues"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Subset 2 applies zero tolerance for unobserved pocket residues: all mapped binding residues must be fully observed in the candidate structure, enabling more confident geometric comparisons.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Subset 2 applies the same resolution and diffraction-method filters as Subset 1 but also requires all mapped binding residues to be observed (observed residues at 100% of MBR, zero tolerance for unobserved pocket residues).</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P042">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P042: Subset2 - enables_comparison_of - Pocket_RMSD</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Subset2"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/enables_comparison_of"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Pocket_RMSD"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Because Subset 2 requires all pocket residues to be observed, it enables more confident pocket-level geometric comparisons including pocket RMSD between apo and holo entries.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Here the entire query pocket is mapped and observed fully in both apo and holo candidates which enables more confident pocket-related observations. A difference of 40% is observed in pocket RMSD.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P043">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P043: Subset1 - has_apo_form_for - UniProt_accession</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Subset1"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/has_apo_form_for"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/UniProt_accession"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>In Subset 1, 39% of UniProt ACs (10,595 out of 27,162) have at least one apo state in the PDB, showing that apo structures are available for a substantial but minority share of represented proteins.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>In UniProt ACs this translated to 39% having at least one apo state in the PDB (10,595 UniProt ACs).</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       CLAIMS — Interface_pocket as subject
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P044">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P044: Interface_pocket - requires - Residue_mapping</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Interface_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/requires"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Residue_mapping"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Interface pockets forming at two or more chain boundaries require residue mapping across all contributing chains and are explicitly included in AHoJ-DB, referred to as relevant chains.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>We also include binding pockets that form at the interface of multiple chains. Analysis of the database shows that about 24% of the binding sites occur at the interface of two or more chains.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/ahoj/claim/P045">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/ontology/ClaimTriplet"/>
    <rdfs:label>P045: Interface_pocket - can_occur_at - Apo_form</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Article_AHoJDB_2024"/>
    <ahoj:claimSubject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Interface_pocket"/>
    <ahoj:claimPredicate rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/predicate/can_occur_at"/>
    <ahoj:claimObject rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/ahoj/entity/Apo_form"/>
    <ahoj:claimText>Interface binding pockets, which occur at the boundary of two or more chains, can also appear in apo form and are annotated as such in AHoJ-DB.</ahoj:claimText>
    <ahoj:sourceText>Analysis of the database shows that about 24% of the binding sites occur at the interface of two or more chains and that less than 50% of the total binding sites processed have an apo form in the PDB.</ahoj:sourceText>
  </rdf:Description>

